\section{A normal-symbol criterion for lower-dimensional modular reduction}\label{20002507}

\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


\paragraph{Definitions and notation.}A modular or automorphic form transforms according to a specified group and weight on a parameter domain. Moduli of Calabi--Yau threefolds parametrize their geometric structures. Reduction to lower-dimensional forms may mean restriction, factorization or descent under a map of moduli spaces, and these are different operations.

\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
When can modular forms on Calabi Yau 3-folds moduli spaces be reduced to lower dimensional forms?
\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}The source leaves the modular group, domain, notion of form and reduction map unspecified. The problem is to supply and investigate a meaningful reduction theory, not assert all such objects factor. The catalogue title/status was paired with a machine-generated research attempt; that attempt is not peer-reviewed evidence of settlement. The statement covers the original source questions rather than only the title’s subcase.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine when modular objects on Calabi–Yau threefold moduli can reduce to lower-dimensional forms.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Proved a local connected-fiber descent criterion and explicit counterexamples to restriction or finite-jet tests. No global Calabi-Yau automorphic reduction is established.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{A local descent test.}Let a moduli chart be a product \(U\times V\), with complex coordinates \(z\) on \(U\) and \(w_1,\ldots,w_r\) on connected \(V\), and let the proposed reduction be projection to \(U\). In a chosen holomorphic trivialization, a holomorphic section \(F(z,w)\) descends to a function of \(z\) exactly when \(\partial_{w_j}F=0\) for every \(j\). Necessity follows by differentiation. For sufficiency, vanishing derivatives makes \(F\) locally constant along each fiber, hence constant on the connected fiber; its value at a fixed \(w_0\) gives a holomorphic descended function.
\paragraph{Restriction is weaker.}For \(F(z,w)=f(z)+w g(z)\), restriction to \(w=0\) is \(f\), regardless of \(g\), while descent fails whenever \(g\ne0\). Thus checking only the restricted Fourier series or value on a lower-dimensional locus cannot prove reduction. The first normal derivative detects this example. Vanishing finitely many normal derivatives is also insufficient: \(f(z)+w^{m+1}g(z)\) passes every test through order \(m\).
\paragraph{Automorphic compatibility and gap.}A modular section transforms with a multiplier or line-bundle automorphy factor. Even fiberwise constancy in one trivialization is not globally meaningful unless that factor descends along the proposed map. Establishing group compatibility, transition functions and behavior at singular fibers is the first missing global step. No actual Calabi--Yau moduli space or modular object is supplied by this local criterion. It distinguishes restriction from descent and gives explicit failed finite-jet tests, but does not classify the reductions requested in the exploratory source programme.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked connectedness, local holomorphic trivialization, derivative sufficiency, finite-jet counterexamples and global multiplier caveat. This was an LLM adversarial audit, not independent human verification; no theorem prover was used. Prompts A then B applied to this ID: Checked connectedness, local holomorphic trivialization, derivative sufficiency, finite-jet counterexamples and global multiplier caveat. Scope remains limited: Identify a compatible moduli map and descending automorphy bundle, then control singular fibers and global monodromy.

Coordinator checked the local connected-fiber descent criterion and failure of every fixed finite-jet test. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 and local shell used to read supplied files and serialize this manuscript; mathematics derived symbolically in the text, with no numerical experiment or formal proof assistant. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
\textbf{UNRESOLVED}. Identify a compatible moduli map and descending automorphy bundle, then control singular fibers and global monodromy.

